Simon says: 
>Does anyone have the details on how to train pets and what they will do  
>for you?  That is a trick I've never learned- I've just dealt with the  
>Keystone Kops and mollified my god with sacrifices in the past...

Sure.  Somebody might call this a spoiler...

First, you need something that pets like to eat.  Tripe is best.  You
can try with food rations, or light corpses, but I stick to tripe.

As you probably know, the square immediately inside the door of a shop
is not part of the shop as far as ownership of stuff goes.  If you drop
things there, the shopkeeper won't try to buy them, and if you pick
something up from there, you don't have to pay.

So the key is to get your pet to pick stuff up from the shop and drop
it there, while you stand in the doorway.  Then grab it.

This takes forever.  But pets are smart.  If a pet drops an object in
this square, immediately throw a tripe ration to the pet while it's
still standing there.  Your pet will immediately grab more stuff and
drop it there.  If you have excess tripe, repeat the trick at the
right time.  Your pet is now a stealing machine, grabbing every 
uncursed, light enough object in the vicinity and bringing it to you.
Scoop 'em up.  Sell 'em back.  Repeat.  You may have to get the stuff
on the far side of the shop and bring it closer to the door, then let
your pet take it from there.

Oh, large dogs and cats can carry more than small ones.  Fill up a 
chest and let Rover carry a whole pile of stuff to you at once.  
Though I find that once the pet is trained, a huge shop can be cleaned
out in no time.  It's the time waiting for the pet to steal one item
on its own that's a pain.  Make sure you're unburdened, so your 
pet can't drop something and then hop out of tripe range while you 
sit.  A magic whistle speeds the process up a bit, when Rover is 
hanging out on the far side of the store.

I really like this method, as it doesn't anger shopkeepers or gods
and it guarantees uncursed items.  Plus, you can sell the stuff 
back a few times and have enough cash to buy anything that your
pet can't or won't bring you.

Unfortunately, it seems sometimes that I spend more time manipulating
my inventory in this way than actually playing the game.  But it's
worth it in the long run, as getting every item in the shops and 
the mines usually results in some nice toys and gets most stuff
identified.

